 of the industries within  which those jobs exist when you look at  sort of the education level required for  many of those jobs it's easy to see how  the the jobs that are less likely to be  automatable by and large sort of holding  other factors equal our jobs that  require more education so that seems to  me Oh more like a very direct way in  which if you start to see technology  favor maintaining jobs that require more  education that could become quite a  self-fulfilling process which will  increase inequality drastically  absolutely I agree I would just add  maybe a distinction that is important to  make between the level of technology so  you have the new technology that  replaces an unskilled or a less skilled  worker so sometimes it could also be a  skilled worker some of the technology  today replaces a lot of they work the  doctors to medical doctors but let's say  but by and large this is not the group  that is mostly effective the dental is  the rate of change except because a lot  of technologies when they're first  introduced they require a skilled worker  to implement to work with but over the  time think of word processors that say  stuff like that the new technology  becomes very friendly and less skilled  workers could implement could use it so  what happens is that the rate of change  the the fact that we all the time are  exposed to more to new a new stuff  arriving you need the skilled workers  the high ability workers at this end of  implementing the new technology until it  becomes a more friendly to the less  skilled workers and I think that's a  major source of deriving inequality well  along those lines could we it is it  reasonable to think that  there could be a technological  advancement that simultaneously  increases our our total level of  inequality but also increases welfare  for most if not all people in other  words is it possible that we might be  able to raise the standard of living for  everybody to some degree however we  might also increase inequality as a  result of that technology at the same  time absolutely I think that this is  what happens in a flock most of the last  a decade it varies between countries so  if you think of the United States there  was a slowdown it's not even there  reduction in wage earnings or in the  lower part of the distribution but most  developed countries this is not the case  Mike my home country Israel for instance  inequality has increased but the the  welfare of the lower-income earners the  less skilled workers has also increased  now there is a bit of a philosophical  question here is about how do you  measure welfare because if you think  we're welfare is of course the bundle of  consumption I could afford and having  all these innovation increased the wages  of even the less skilled workers and  increase the variety of consumption good  is that the technology we use is also  part of our consumption the smartphone's  we have helped us a lot in anything we  could waste time on Facebook and stuff  like that and enjoy so that's one side  of it but then there is the inequality  and if my welfare or one welfare is  negatively affected by Envy by  understanding that other people income  growth is much faster then you could  easily get the result in which the  welfare of the lower tail in the income  distribution actually goes down this is  sort of difficult to measure in a sort  of net in  for economists isn't it I mean imagine  for example some significant medical  technology that eliminates or eradicate  I don't know some disease that affects  people regardless of income so that's a  really good thing for welfare but at the  same time some people are enriched by  that and inequality in cities becomes  more noticeable and we know that that  connects to more petty crime on the  street right how could we even get a  sense of sort of the net impact of that  I think it's very hard to get the sense  of the net the impact of course you  could measure a crime 